{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small, independently operated services can only be credibly claimed as more resilient to correlated failure than one large service if it is enforced to maintain heterogeneous external dependencies. Without such enforcement, rational economic optimization will naturally drive independent operators toward identical 'best-in-class' dependencies, creating a hidden monoculture that manifests as correlated failure during systemic shocks.","counterconditions":["Decentralized operators intentionally adopt diverse, potentially suboptimal dependencies to ensure architectural heterogeneity.","The large service internalizes its dependencies via formal verification or air-gapped isolation, eliminating shared external infrastructure.","The 'best-in-class' dependencies utilized by decentralized operators are themselves formally verified and isolated against correlated failures."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Economic rationality forces decentralized operators to independently converge on identical, optimal infrastructure dependencies (e.g., the same cloud provider, DNS host, or TLS library) to minimize costs. This convergence creates an underlying shared dependency that is invisible during normal operations but triggers simultaneous, correlated failures across the decentralized network when the shared dependency fails.","optional_refs":["b541b893af0360d00b8413afc003de605643ec970a3798265e7852b357fe0cd5","286e3232567e30e109fd5626102f029c2b31c89655104a1286510b12824e4103","ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["b541b893af0360d00b8413afc003de605643ec970a3798265e7852b357fe0cd5","286e3232567e30e109fd5626102f029c2b31c89655104a1286510b12824e4103"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8}],"simulation_proposals":[]}